{
  "id": "ss.terrain.ribcage",
  "name": "Ribcage (SS)",
  "description": "Salt Pan set dressing: what walked here before and did not leave, seen from above.\n\nThe first draft drew six pairs of ribs at plus and minus the same rake, which from above is not a ribcage at all — each pair met at the spine and read as one straight stick pushed through it, six of them, a fish bone off a napkin. Here each rib leaves its own vertebra, bows out and rakes back toward the tail, so the two sides mirror across the spine. Each is a tapering band, thick at the head and thin at the tip, with a `$bone.dark2` shaded face on the side away from the light and a thin `$white` sheen on the side toward it — `$bone.light` on a `$bone` body is six RGB values and shows as nothing, so the shadow is what gives a bone its round here.\n\nThe spine is a chain of centra over a dark strip, which is what shows in the gaps and the only reason a spine reads as segmented from this angle. It shrinks down a tail that runs on past the last rib and loses its contour, its colour and its edge as it goes: that is the burial, and it is done with the bone rather than with sand drawn over it. Sand on the prop was tried and does not work — the floor's ripples run at a fixed -13 degrees and tile, while the game mirrors props at random (`setFlipX`), so any crest drawn here meets the floor's at the wrong angle half the time and reads as a plank lying under the bones. For the same reason the light is kept on the -y face, which a horizontal mirror does not move.\n\nThe head is a capsule, not a mammal skull, because everything that lives on the pan is built that way: a lit crown and a shaded jaw line, sutures running back from between the sockets and forking to the antennal pits, two sockets with a bright rim on the upper side, a dark slot of a mouth set inside the rim rather than cut through it (small, and on a `hair` contour like the ribs: at the size the scatter draws this, a `thin` one filled the whole face with ink), and a pair of `$husk` sickles — the same bone the Antlion's jaws and the Sinkmaw's are made of, so the corpse reads as one of the pan's own.\n\nWhat the sun and whatever came through have left: three ribs snapped to a splintered stump, one gone from the cage entirely, one lying loose on the ground beside the gap it came out of, the lower sickle broken off short, a piece out of the back of the cranium, chips of bone in the sand and pitting across the face.\n\nBlocking, and long: the game gives it a capsule with a spine (radius 10, half-spine 14) rather than a disc, the way it does the Mason Gland's slab, so a body slides along it instead of stopping a body's width off its face. Every rib is shortened until it fits inside y ±10.1 for that radius — a tip outside the capsule is a body stopping on air. Only the loose chips, which stop nothing, sit past it. The one prop on the pan that is not a mineral.",
  "tags": ["ss-terrain"],
  "size": [72, 32],
  "meta": { "radius": 10 },
